# function fill(grid: number[][], v: number): number { for (let y = 0; y < grid.length; y++) for (let x = 0; x < grid[y].length; x++) grid[y][x] = v + x; return grid[0][0]; }
# print(fill(id([[1.5, 2.5], [3.5]]), id(0.25)));
# Every turn of the inner loop reads grid[y] anew and stores into it. The row was read in front of a typed copy only where the turns store no element at all, and a store into a list that a read in the
# turn finds counted as other code: no typed copy was even tried: 49 instructions a turn against 10 with the row taken out by hand; `grid[y][x] += 0.5` 127 against 14.
# A raw double that a typed store lays into a list cannot be the row that was read off a list of cells: one list has not both storages unless it is converted between, and that writes its storage. The
# mask of the elements of lists carries a mark, read or written, and the writes of the two typed stores of raw doubles alone lack it: the row moves to the front where no turn writes the mark.
# Where every row of the loop leads from in front, the front reads it once, asks it as any list that leads from in front, for storage that takes numbers by its kind, and that it is not the list of rows itself.
